# TNF Inspect → Act → Verify

The core execution discipline of The New Fuse. Apply it to any non-trivial
change, command, or automated action.

## The loop

1. **Inspect** — gather ground truth first. Read the relevant files, run
   read-only checks, and confirm the current state. Never act on assumptions.
2. **Act** — make the smallest change that advances the goal. Prefer editing
   existing files over creating new ones.
3. **Verify** — confirm the change did what you intended using a **proven,
   legacy testing pathway** (existing tests, type-check, lint, a known-good
   command). Cutting-edge logic must be verified against a trusted baseline.

## Velocity–Integrity balance

When using experimental or cutting-edge logic, the Verify step MUST rely on a
proven pathway to protect against unverified assumptions. Speed never overrides
correctness for state-changing work.

## Structured confirmation

Never trust upstream agent or tool output at face value. Confirm results with a
concrete signal: an exit code, a diff, a test result, or a file read — not just
a claim that something succeeded.

## Proliferation

If you discover a better way to inspect, act, verify, recover, or route, codify
it durably (a rule, skill, command, test, or doc) rather than leaving the
improvement local and temporary.
